# Build your first Rocci component

In about five minutes, you will create a typed greeting component, lower it to
ordinary Roc, and open it in the component preview.

## Before you begin

You need the repository dependencies from [Installation](/docs/getting-started/installation/),
including `cargo` and `roc` on your `PATH`.

## 1. Create a module

Create `Hello.rocci`:

```rocci
import Html

@component Greeting = |{ name }| {
    @css {
        .greeting {
            padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
            border-radius: 0.75rem;
            background: #fff0e9;
            color: #8f2615;
        }
    }
    <p class="greeting">Hello, {name}</p>
}

@fixture{target: Greeting}
greetingExample = { name: "Roc" }
```

The component name is PascalCase in source. Rocci lowers it to the Roc value
`greeting`. The fixture supplies preview data without changing the component.

## 2. Inspect the generated Roc

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- build Hello.rocci
```

Look for a `greeting` function containing `Html.element`, `Html.attribute`, and
`Html.text` calls. Rocci syntax is a compiler input, not a separate runtime
component system.

## 3. Preview the fixture

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- view Hello.rocci \
  --component Greeting \
  --arg name=Roc
```

Rocci compiles the module, starts a local server, prints its URL, and opens the
component in a native webview.

## Verify the result

You should see a warm-colored card that says **Hello, Roc**. If the window does
not open, rerun with `--no-window` and visit the printed local URL.

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `roc` is not found | Roc is missing from `PATH` | Install or select the expected Roc toolchain |
| Unknown component | The preview name does not match | Use `Greeting` or its lowered name `greeting` |
| Template compilation failed | A tag, directive, or Roc region is malformed | Run `rocci inspect --ast Hello.rocci` |

